#
# The applications this effect recognizes, and what it does for each of them.
#
# This file is the effect's own defaults. It is installed with the package,
# replaced whenever the package is updated, and never written to. A user's own
# entries and changes live in the file of the same name under their
# configuration directory, where KConfig layers them over these: a field the
# user did not change keeps following this file, so a later package delivers
# new applications and corrected methods without touching what they edited.
#
# Every field below was read off a running instance of the stated version. A
# name never implies an identity, and a method is not a preference but a
# statement about how that program decides what to render, taken from its
# source and then confirmed by running it. Setting a method a program does not
# follow does not make it render less; it can stop its window covering the
# screen.
#
# WindowClass and Instance identify the window, and each one stated has to
# match exactly. Program identifies the connection before any window exists,
# which is when the request has to be made, and is compared by file name only.
#
# There is one Method entry per way the application can present itself, and
# each is a separate measurement: MethodWaylandFullScreen,
# MethodWaylandBorderless, MethodWaylandWindowed, MethodX11FullScreen,
# MethodX11Borderless and MethodX11Windowed. An entry that is absent means Auto
# - nobody has run the application that way yet - which is not the same as Off,
# which records that the question was asked and the answer is to ask for
# nothing. Each name below acts on one protocol only: the advertisements reach
# a native Wayland client, and the resize reaches an X11 window.
#
# Method is one of:
#   Auto                   Work it out and verify the result.
#   Off                    Recognize it, ask for nothing.
#   AdvertisedMode         Tell it alone that its screen has a smaller mode.
#                          For clients that select a mode and present it
#                          through a viewport still covering the screen.
#   AdvertisedScale        Tell it alone that its screen has a smaller scale.
#                          For clients that render the logical size times the
#                          scale they were told. Only whole steps of the
#                          output's scale are reachable, so an unscaled screen
#                          offers such a client nothing.
#   AdvertisedModeAndScale Both together, for clients that take their size from
#                          the mode but declare the output's scale themselves.
#   X11Resize              Resize a selected X11 fullscreen window. Requires
#                          a client which follows resizing and RandR modes.
#
# Every other setting the effect has may also appear here, and each one absent
# follows the global value the user chose. This file states only what was
# measured about the application, never what somebody would prefer: a
# Resolution here would pin that game and stop the user's own global setting
# ever reaching it. The two benchmarks are the exception and state Native,
# because a benchmark exists to measure a machine and reporting a number for a
# reduced one is wrong whatever anybody prefers. That is a fact about the
# program, not a taste.

[General]
FormatVersion=1

[Application-supertuxkart]
Name=SuperTuxKart
MeasuredVersion=1.4
WindowClass=supertuxkart
Instance=supertuxkart
Program=supertuxkart
MethodWaylandFullScreen=AdvertisedMode
Order=10
Note=Follows the advertised screen mode with its OpenGL renderer. Its Vulkan renderer keeps the full resolution.

[Application-extremetuxracer]
Name=Extreme Tux Racer
MeasuredVersion=0.8.4
# Its window class carries its version number, so matching the class would
# stop matching at the next package. The instance name is the stable field.
Instance=etr
Program=etr
MethodX11FullScreen=X11Resize
X11PrimaryOutputOnly=true
Order=20
Note=Follows X11 resize requests on the primary display. Its SFML mode selection always uses that display, so requests on other displays are refused. Requested sizes must match an available mode.

[Application-hl2_linux]
Name=Left 4 Dead 2 and other native Source games
MeasuredVersion=Steam build 23990068
# hl2_linux is the Source engine binary, not the game: every native Source
# title presents this class and instance, so this entry cannot be made specific
# to one of them. The window title does name the game, but a title carries
# save-game and player names and is never used as an identity here.
WindowClass=hl2_linux
Instance=hl2_linux
Program=hl2_linux
MethodX11FullScreen=X11Resize
Order=50
Note=Takes its fullscreen size from the window manager and never asks Xwayland for a mode, so the effect presents the resized window itself and maps pointer input to it. Measured through Steam's pressure-vessel runtime.

[Application-glmark2]
Name=glmark2
MeasuredVersion=2023.01
WindowClass=com.github.glmark2.glmark2
Instance=glmark2-wayland
Program=glmark2-wayland
MethodWaylandFullScreen=AdvertisedScale
# A benchmark measures a machine. Reducing what it renders without being asked
# would make it report a number for something nobody chose.
Resolution=Native
Order=30
Note=Follows the advertised screen scale. A screen without a scale above one offers it no reduction.

[Application-vkmark]
Name=vkmark
MeasuredVersion=2025.01
WindowClass=com.github.vkmark.vkmark
Instance=vkmark
Program=vkmark
MethodWaylandFullScreen=AdvertisedModeAndScale
Resolution=Native
Order=40
Note=Follows the advertised screen mode and scale together. A screen without a scale above one offers it no reduction.
